feat(vrm): power the touch rails through RPMh, one rail per command

The S3908 on SE13 answers only when pm8150_l13 and pm8150a_l1 are up, and both
are RPMh-owned VREG resources: EE0 must not write them over SPMI, so the
enable has to go out as a TCS command to the APPS RSC.  The state machine that
forms such a command already existed as the host-tested module
package/KebabRpmhVrm.c, but 0128 had deleted the kernel-side draft and left a
comment, so nothing in the image could reach it.  This restores the production
backend and gives it a console command.

  - bsd/dev/kebab_rpmh_vrm.{c,h} are generated from package/KebabRpmhVrm.{c,h}
    by scripts/sync-rpmh-vrm.py, so the kernel runs the exact code the 70 host
    checks drive instead of a second draft that can drift; the header's
    KEBAB_RPMH_KERNEL path selects <sys/types.h> and <libkern/libkern.h>.
  - bsd/dev/kebab_spmi.c binds the module's kebab_tcs_io to the TCS1 window of
    the APPS RSC, at the geometry 0127 measured rather than re-derived:
    drv-2 + 0xd00, 672 bytes per TCS, TCS1 only, IRQ trio at tcs_base
    +0x0/0x4/0x8.  The io refuses any offset outside one TCS window, and the
    window is mapped once at init so a command from the panic loop only reads a
    pointer.
  - the module's one-shot gate is now per command address instead of per boot:
    ldoa13 and ldoc1 are separate ENABLE registers and the console enables them
    in two steps, but a repeat of either rail is still refused, and a rail that
    failed closes the channel for the boot.  Two host checks cover the permitted
    two-step sequence and the failed-first-rail case.
  - VROMON sends ldoa13 and nothing else on the first invocation; VROMON ldoc1
    is the second step and is refused until ldoa13 completed; anything else that
    starts with VROMON is answered with the whitelist.  Both forms are gated by
    the boot argument kebab_vrm_write (default off), so an unattended boot never
    powers a rail.
  - TCS? dumps CONTROL, CMD_ENABLE, CMD_WAIT and CMD_STATUS for TCS0 and TCS1
    plus the IRQ trio, read-only, so a wedged or dirty channel can be told apart
    from a rail that simply did not come up.  It never clears, writes or
    recovers anything: a TCS may still hold another execution environment's
    resource vote.
  - PWR? already read both rails' EN 0x46 through the SPMI observer; it is the
    verification step between the two VROMON invocations and needed no change.

Measured on hardware (boot_b, kebab_vrm_write=1): PWR? read ldoa13 0x00 and
ldoc1 0x00; VROMON moved ldoa13 to 0x80 and left ldoc1 at 0x00; VROMON ldoc1
then moved ldoc1 to 0x80; the SE13 one-byte write to 0x4b, which aborted with
state 0x21 before, completed with GENI_STATUS 0x00000000 and printed ACK.  TCS?
showed TCS1 idle and clean, and TCS0 still holding another execution
environment's leftover command, which was left untouched.

